Subject: [PATCH] Fail fast: exit if IMPSystem init fails instead of running streamless (C2)
Target: prudynt-t @ f4b32289359d176fb0795d9f3735af0bd9d6d2fd (branch stable).
Stacks on: 0001-0009. Applies with git apply -p1 / patch -p1 AFTER them. Touches only
main.cpp, in a region no other patch modifies.

main() calls imp_system = IMPSystem::createNew() but never checks the result. createNew()
catches the constructor exception thrown when IMPSystem::init() fails (IMP_ISP_Open,
add_sensor_with_retry, enable_sensor, IMP_System_Init, IMP_ISP_EnableTuning, set_running_mode)
and returns nullptr. On a cold or power-cut boot any of those steps can fail. With no null
check, main() proceeds to spawn the audio/video/RTSP workers on an uninitialised IMP: the
encoder never produces SPS/PPS, RTSP registers no channel, and the process stays ALIVE but
permanently STREAMLESS. An OPTIONS-only watchdog reads that as healthy (the RTSP socket still
answers OPTIONS=200) and never restarts it -> dead stream until manual intervention. This is
the worst class of failure for a life-safety camera.

Fix: if IMPSystem::createNew() returns null, log and return 1 (mirroring the existing
time-sync failure path two blocks up: join the shutdown-signal thread, then return). Exiting
drops the RTSP socket, so the init system / S32prudyntwd watchdog detects a down socket and
restarts a fresh, warm process - the proven recovery. Fail-fast turns an invisible
streamless state into a recoverable one. No new state; the InstanceLock is released by its
RAII guard on return.

Caveat (honest scope): this catches only the case where createNew() returns null. At DEBUG
loglevel the IMPSystem constructor SUPPRESSES its throw (init() != 0 && level != DEBUG), and
the sensor-resolution-0x0 path "continues anyway" without flagging an error - so createNew()
can return a NON-null but half-initialised object that this check waves through. Those
residual cases are recovered by 0011 (C5: zero video channels register -> SIGTERM ->
watchdog restart), which is the required backstop. The durable fix for the non-null-broken
cases is upstream in IMPSystem's constructor/init (drop the DEBUG-level throw gate; make the
sensor-0x0 path set a non-zero result) and is out of scope here.

diff --git a/src/main.cpp b/src/main.cpp
index 11499e5..74961e1 100644
--- a/src/main.cpp
+++ b/src/main.cpp
@@ -606,6 +606,21 @@ int main(int argc, const char *argv[]) {
     imp_system = IMPSystem::createNew();
   }
 
+  // C2: fail-fast on IMP init failure. IMPSystem::createNew() returns null when
+  // any IMP bring-up step fails (IMP_ISP_Open, add/enable sensor,
+  // IMP_System_Init, EnableTuning ...), which is most likely on a cold or
+  // power-cut boot. Continuing would spawn the audio/video/RTSP workers on an
+  // uninitialised IMP -> the encoder never produces SPS/PPS -> RTSP registers
+  // no channel -> the process stays alive but permanently streamless, which an
+  // OPTIONS-only watchdog reads as healthy and never recovers. Exiting instead
+  // drops the RTSP socket, so the supervisor/watchdog restarts a fresh (warm)
+  // process - the proven recovery.
+  if (!imp_system) {
+    LOG_ERROR("IMPSystem initialisation failed; exiting for supervisor restart");
+    join_signal_thread(true);
+    return 1;
+  }
+
   bool mic_is_digital = cfg && cfg->audio.mic_is_digital;
   int audio_input_device_id = mic_is_digital ? 0 : 1;
   const char *cpu_info = (cfg->sysinfo.cpu && cfg->sysinfo.cpu[0] != '\0')
